QUEST='https://www.sos.mo.gov/archives/exhibits/quest/facilities/'
PLACES=[
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Iowa
N('mount-pleasant-state-hospital-ia','Mount Pleasant State Hospital (Iowa Lunatic Asylum)','Mount Pleasant','ia','inst','standing',40.95578,-91.5361,'site','view','drive','','1855–2015',
  "Iowa's first state asylum, built on the Kirkbride plan between 1855 and 1865 for about $400,000; the first patient came in February 1861. The Kirkbride Buildings site names Jonathan Preston of Boston as the architect and calls the design a modest Elizabethan style. A fire in 1936 destroyed most of the administration section, leaving a kitchen block at the back, and the state put up a newer building on that spot. The Mental Health Institute shared the campus with the Mount Pleasant Correctional Facility and closed in June 2015.",
  "How much of the old asylum survives is not settled: the Kirkbride Buildings site says there is hardly anything left of the original building and that the wings came down later in the twentieth century, while a 2018 newspaper report says inmates are housed in the original hospital building. The same report describes a small museum of the hospital's history opened in 2018 in the former Mental Health Institute building on the prison grounds, free and open on alternate Fridays; whether it still opens was not checked.",
  '','The grounds are the Mount Pleasant Correctional Facility, an active state prison on Hospital Avenue. Look from the public road only; ask the prison about the museum before going.',
  [W('Mount Pleasant Mental Health Institute'),['Kirkbride Buildings: Mount Pleasant State Hospital',KB+'mountpleasant/'],['Southeast Iowa Union: museum for MHI opening on grounds of correctional facility (2018)','https://www.southeastiowaunion.com/news/museum-for-mhi-opening-on-grounds-of-correctional-facility-in-mt-pleasant'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Jonathan Preston',archw='Kirkbride Buildings site: "designed in a relatively modest Elizabethan style by architect Jonathan Preston" of Boston'),
N('independence-state-hospital-ia','Independence State Hospital (Independence Mental Health Institute)','Independence','ia','inst','standing',42.45705,-91.92646,'site','ask','drive','','1873–',
  "Iowa's second asylum, funded by the legislature on April 6, 1868 to relieve the crowded hospital at Mount Pleasant. It took ten years and $845,000 to finish, and the first patients came on May 1, 1873. The Kirkbride-plan building was designed by Stephen Vaughn Shipman, who also designed the asylums at Elgin and Anna in Illinois and Oshkosh and Mendota in Wisconsin, and the grounds, roads and lakes were laid out by the landscape designer J. J. Weidenmann. It is still a working state psychiatric hospital.",
  "The Kirkbride building stands at the end of a long lawn and looks much as it does in early photographs, with mansard roofs, arched windows, bracketed eaves, domes, corner towers and roof turrets. Its walls are of stone from the quarries around Farley and Stone City, on a foundation of prairie granite. A museum of the hospital's past, \"Days of Yore\", is inside and is shown on tours.",
  '','A working state hospital (the Mental Health Institute, Iowa Department of Health and Human Services), 2277 Iowa Avenue. Tours for schools, groups and organizations are arranged in advance with the institute and can include the museum (Buchanan County history site). Otherwise look from the road.',
  [W('Independence State Hospital'),['Buchanan County history: Mental Health Institute','https://www.buchanancountyhistory.com/mental-health-institute/'],['Kirkbride Buildings: Independence State Hospital',KB+'independence/'],KBL],
  brick='no',arch='Stephen Vaughn Shipman',archw='Wikipedia and the Kirkbride Buildings site: "The architect was Stephen Vaughn Shipman"'),
N('clarinda-state-hospital-ia','Clarinda State Hospital (Clarinda Treatment Complex)','Clarinda','ia','inst','standing',40.75835,-95.03599,'site','view','drive','','1885–2015',
  "Iowa's third asylum. A state commission chose Clarinda in 1884, the cornerstone was laid on July 4, 1885, and the first patients, all men because the women's wing was not finished, came in 1888. The architects were William Foster and Henry F. Liebbe of Des Moines; the Kirkbride building encloses more than 500,000 square feet behind a restrained Gothic front. The Mental Health Institute closed on June 30, 2015.",
  "The Kirkbride building stands, red brick with white trim, with a large clock tower over the administration centre; many windows have been replaced, but original woodwork and tile floors were kept inside. In 2016 the Associated Press found much of it empty, with parts leased to a drug and alcohol treatment centre and to dormitories for youth, and its basement kitchens and maintenance shared with the Clarinda Correctional Facility next door. Wikipedia names Clarinda Academy as the only tenant; neither source read is recent.",
  '','State property beside the Clarinda Correctional Facility, a medium-security prison, on North 16th Street; prisoners walk the grounds. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Clarinda Treatment Complex'),['Kirkbride Buildings: Clarinda State Hospital',KB+'clarinda/index.html'],['Associated Press (Washington Times): Clarinda struggles to fill 128-year-old former hospital (2016)','https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2016/feb/5/clarinda-struggles-to-fill-128-year-old-former-hos/'],KBL],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='William Foster; Henry F. Liebbe',archw='Wikipedia and the Kirkbride Buildings site: "William Foster and Henry F. Liebbe of Des Moines were retained as architects"'),
N('cherokee-state-hospital-ia','Cherokee State Hospital (Cherokee Mental Health Institute)','Cherokee','ia','inst','standing',42.75726,-95.57278,'site','view','drive','','1902–',
  "Iowa's fourth asylum, for the northwest of the state. The legislature took 14 ballots in 1894 to choose Cherokee, and it was six and a half years from the first digging before the administration building, on bare prairie, was ready. It opened on August 15, 1902 as the Cherokee Lunatic Asylum; within days 306 patients came by special train from Independence and 252 from Clarinda. The Kirkbride Buildings site names Henry F. Liebbe of Des Moines as the architect. It is still a state psychiatric hospital.",
  "The long Kirkbride building stands and the hospital uses much of it, but it has changed: the cupola over the administration centre is gone and the slate roof was replaced with corrugated metal. Most of the south wing holds the state's civil commitment unit for sex offenders behind prison-grade fencing. The Kirkbride Buildings site says a museum of the asylum's history in the basement opened by appointment.",
  '','A working state facility (the Cherokee Mental Health Institute), 1251 West Cedar Loop, with a fenced secure unit. Look from the public road only; the museum, if it still opens, is by appointment with the institute.',
  [W('Cherokee Mental Health Institute'),['Kirkbride Buildings: Cherokee State Hospital',KB+'cherokee/'],KBL],
  brick='?',arch='Henry F. Liebbe',archw='Kirkbride Buildings site: architect Henry F. Liebbe of Des Moines'),
N('glenwood-state-school-ia','Glenwood State Hospital-School (Iowa Institution for Feeble-Minded Children)','Glenwood','ia','inst','standing',41.03861,-95.73728,'site','view','drive','','1876–2024',
  "In March 1876 the legislature put Iowa's first asylum for \"feeble-minded\" children on the grounds of the Civil War orphans' home at Glenwood, and it opened on September 1, 1876. An administration building went up in 1884, and the institution grew to more than 1,000 acres and almost 2,000 residents by 1952, becoming the Glenwood State Hospital-School and later the Glenwood Resource Center. The federal Justice Department found that conditions there violated residents' constitutional rights, and the state closed it in 2024.",
  "The campus of more than 100 buildings passed to the Glenwood Redevelopment Corporation, which in August 2026 was pulling down about 90 percent of them to make ground ready for housing. About ten are being kept, among them an administration building, the Meyer Building, the chapel, the former fire station and the girls' and boys' cottages; the sources read do not say whether that administration building is the one of 1884. A four-storey building at 103 Central was renovated in 2025 as a child care centre and school district offices.",
  '','Land of the Glenwood Redevelopment Corporation, largely closed to the public since October 2025 while demolition goes on. Look from the public road.',
  [W('Glenwood Resource Center'),['Glenwood Opinion-Tribune: redevelopment of former GRC campus (August 2026)','https://opinion-tribune.com/node/8004'],['KJAN: major building on former state property renovated (2025)','https://www.kjan.com/?p=546792']],
  brick='?'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Missouri
N('fulton-state-hospital-mo','Fulton State Hospital (State Lunatic Asylum No. 1)','Fulton','mo','inst','gone',38.84652,-91.93706,'area','view','drive','','1851–',
  "Missouri's first public asylum, set up by the General Assembly in 1847 and opened in 1851: the first public asylum west of the Mississippi. The first building, three storeys with 72 rooms, was a small Kirkbride-plan house designed in 1849 by Solomon Jenkins. It closed in 1861 for the Civil War, when Union troops used it as a barracks, and reopened on September 7, 1863; from the late 1880s Morris Frederick Bell rebuilt and enlarged it. A fire in March 1956 destroyed the 105-year-old administration building and the wings beside it, and a new administration building opened in 1958; the Missouri State Archives give the date of the fire as March 14, Asylum Projects as March 15.",
  "The Kirkbride Buildings site says the original building was either torn down completely or altered past recognition. The campus is still Fulton State Hospital, with the Biggs and Guhleman forensic centres and the Nixon Forensic Center, opened in July 2019; the sources read do not say which older buildings survive on it.",
  '','A working state psychiatric hospital with the state\'s only maximum-security psychiatric unit, on State Street. Look from the public road only.',
  [['Kirkbride Buildings: Fulton State Hospital',KB+'fulton/index.html'],['Missouri State Archives: 1855, early obstacles',QUEST+'1855'],['Missouri State Archives: 1956, from the ashes',QUEST+'1956'],['Asylum Projects: Fulton State Hospital (fire date)',AP+'Fulton_State_Hospital'],['Fulton State Hospital rebuild (Missouri)','https://fultonrebuild.mo.gov']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Solomon Jenkins; Morris Frederick Bell',archw='Missouri State Archives: plans signed "Solomon Jenkins, Architect, 1849"; Kirkbride Buildings site: alterations from the late 1880s led by architect Morris Frederick Bell'),
N('st-joseph-state-hospital-mo','St. Joseph State Hospital (State Lunatic Asylum No. 2)','St. Joseph','mo','inst','standing',39.77832,-94.81157,'site','view','drive','','1874–',
  "The General Assembly approved a second state asylum in 1872, on land east of St. Joseph, and State Lunatic Asylum No. 2 opened in November 1874 with 275 beds. A fire in 1879 damaged much of the Kirkbride building; it reopened in 1880. Renamed St. Joseph State Hospital in 1899, it held nearly 3,000 patients in the early 1950s. In the 1990s the old campus became a state prison and a new psychiatric centre opened across the street.",
  "The Kirkbride building was partly pulled down, and what remains, the old administration building among it, is part of the prison. The hospital's history is shown at the Glore Psychiatric Museum just outside the prison gates.",
  '','Inside an active state prison: look from Frederick Avenue only. The museum next door is the way to see the hospital\'s past.',
  [['Kirkbride Buildings: Saint Joseph State Hospital',KB+'saintjoseph/'],['Missouri Department of Mental Health: history of the St. Joseph hospital','https://dmh.mo.gov/nmprc/history'],W('Glore Psychiatric Museum')],
  brick='?',apart='glore-psychiatric-museum-mo: the museum is a separate building outside the prison fence, open to visitors'),
N('glore-psychiatric-museum-mo','Glore Psychiatric Museum','St. Joseph','mo','inst','standing',39.77631,-94.80837,'exact','open','drive','','1967–',
  "A museum of the St. Joseph State Hospital and of the history of treating mental illness, begun in 1966 when George Glore, a state mental health worker, built life-size models of old treatment devices for a Mental Health Awareness Week. It became a museum in 1967 in a ward of the old asylum, and moved, after the asylum became a prison, to a 1968 building outside the prison gates that had been the hospital's medical unit.",
  "Rooms of the hospital's own equipment, uniforms, photographs and patients' art and writing, and full-size models of 16th- to 18th-century devices such as a tranquilizer chair and a treadmill. It is one of four museums on the St. Joseph Museums' Frederick Avenue site, all on one ticket.",
  '','Run by St. Joseph Museums, 3406 Frederick Avenue; one admission covers the four museums on the site (adults $12 when read). Hours from the museums\' own page.',
  [W('Glore Psychiatric Museum'),['St. Joseph Museums: hours and admission','https://www.stjosephmuseum.org/hours-and-admission'],['Missouri Department of Mental Health: Glore Psychiatric Museum','https://dmh.mo.gov/nmprc/glore']],
  brick='?',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed on major holidays',apart='st-joseph-state-hospital-mo: the old asylum is inside the prison next door; this is the museum outside the gates'),
N('nevada-state-hospital-mo','Nevada State Hospital (State Lunatic Asylum No. 3)','Nevada','mo','inst','gone',37.86069,-94.35821,'area','view','drive','','1887–1999',
  "Missouri's third asylum, provided for in 1885. The Kirkbride building, designed by Morris Frederick Bell in the Second Empire style, was finished in 1887; Asylum Projects says the first building opened on October 17, 1889, so the opening year is not settled. Its towers and other ornament were taken off in the twentieth century. The hospital closed in 1991 and the empty Kirkbride building was torn down in 1999.",
  "The Kirkbride building is gone. The 1937 infirmary, a Public Works Administration building in red brick in the Modernist style and on the National Register, stands at 2095 North Ash Street and is now a retirement home. Asylum Projects says about 1,500 patients are buried in the hospital cemetery.",
  '','The pin is on the old hospital grounds by the infirmary, not on the footprint of the Kirkbride building, which was not found. The infirmary is a private retirement home; look from the street.',
  [W('Nevada State Hospital'),['Kirkbride Buildings: Nevada State Hospital',KB+'nevada/'],['National Register record: Infirmary Building, Missouri State Hospital Number 3','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/05001330'],W('Infirmary Building, Missouri State Hospital Number 3'),['Asylum Projects: Nevada State Hospital (opening date, cemetery)',AP+'Nevada_State_Hospital'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Morris Frederick Bell',archw='Kirkbride Buildings site: architect Morris Frederick Bell, a Second Empire Kirkbride building'),
N('farmington-state-hospital-mo','Farmington State Hospital (State Hospital No. 4)','Farmington','mo','inst','standing',37.76985,-90.43486,'site','view','drive','','1903–',
  "Missouri's fourth state hospital, authorized by the General Assembly in 1899 with $150,000 for a site and buildings; the first seven patients came in January 1903. The state's mental health department calls it one of the first cottage-plan institutions in the United States: each cottage had day rooms below and dormitories above. It grew past 1,000 patients and kept its own dairy, cannery, power plant, post office and graveyard. In 1987 the hospital buildings were turned into a prison and a new hospital, the Southeast Missouri Mental Health Center, opened nearby.",
  "The old hospital buildings are part of the prison. The bell that hung over the administration building now stands under the flagpole in the circle in front of the present mental health centre, and the old front-gate guardhouse is hidden in brush at the back of the cemetery.",
  '','The old hospital is an active state prison off West Columbia Street: look from the public road only. The bell is in front of the Southeast Missouri Mental Health Center, also a working state hospital.',
  [['Missouri Department of Mental Health: history of the Southeast Missouri Mental Health Center','https://dmh.mo.gov/smmhc/history']],
  brick='?'),
N('st-louis-insane-asylum-mo','St. Louis County Insane Asylum (City Sanitarium, St. Louis State Hospital)','St. Louis','mo','inst','standing',38.60396,-90.27915,'site','view','drive','','1869–',
  "Built in 1869 on a hilltop off Arsenal Street, then outside the city, on land bought from the short-lived Kemper College; it opened that year with patients brought by wagon train from the crowded state hospital at Fulton. The architect was William Rumbold, who also designed the dome of the Old Courthouse downtown. It became the St. Louis Insane Asylum in 1876, the City Sanitarium in 1910 and a state hospital in 1948; two long wings were added in 1912.",
  "The 1869 main building stands, red brick in the Italianate style with stone quoins, a bracketed cornice and a cast-iron dome and cupola that can be seen for miles; the front portico had marble columns. Several younger buildings on the grounds were pulled down, and a 1962 Modernist building was put in front of the old entrance. A full renovation of the grounds and buildings began in late 1998. It is part of the state's St. Louis Psychiatric Rehabilitation Center and is a city landmark.",
  '','A working state psychiatric hospital owned by the State of Missouri, 5400 Arsenal Street. Look from Arsenal Street; the dome shows from far around.',
  [['Built St. Louis: St. Louis State Hospital','https://builtstlouis.net/statehospital01.html'],['City of St. Louis preservation plan: hospitals and social service buildings','https://www.stlouis-mo.gov/government/departments/planning/cultural-resources/preservation-plan/Part-II-The-Walking-City-Hospitals-Social-Service-Buildings.cfm'],['Missouri Department of Mental Health: history','https://dmh.mo.gov/ftc/history']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='William Rumbold',archw='City of St. Louis preservation plan and Built St. Louis: designed by William Rumbold, architect of the Old Courthouse dome'),
N('st-vincents-hospital-normandy-mo',"St. Vincent's Hospital (St. Vincent's Sanitarium)",'Normandy','mo','inst','standing',38.69261,-90.30667,'site','view','drive','','1895–',
  "A Catholic asylum for nervous and mental diseases, founded by the Daughters of Charity, who opened it in August 1858 at Ninth and Marion Streets in St. Louis. In 1895 they moved to a castle-like building on a hilltop above St. Charles Rock Road, designed by George R. Mann on the Kirkbride plan in the manner of a French Renaissance chateau. The sisters ran it until it closed: the Riverfront Times gives 1978, Wikipedia the 1980s. It was listed on the National Register in 1982.",
  "The building stands and is now the Castle Park Apartments, low-income housing, at 1600 Castle Park Drive. The Register record names Harvey Ellis beside George R. Mann.",
  '','Private apartments: look from Castle Park Drive.',
  [W("St. Vincent's Hospital (Normandy, Missouri)"),['National Register record: St. Vincent\'s Hospital','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/82004722'],['Riverfront Times: the castles of Normandy and Bel-Nor (2014)','https://www.riverfronttimes.com/news/the-castles-of-normandy-and-bel-nor-2577581'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='George R. Mann; Harvey Ellis',archw='National Register record: architects "Mann, George R.; Ellis, Harvey"; Riverfront Times: architect George R. Mann'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Kansas
N('topeka-state-hospital-ks','Topeka State Hospital (Topeka Insane Asylum) site and cemetery','Topeka','ks','inst','gone',39.06774,-95.70797,'site','view','drive','','1879–1997',
  "Kansas's second state asylum, after Osawatomie. Both were first designed by John G. Haskell, one of the architects of the Kansas capitol, and Topeka was built on the Kirkbride plan on 80 donated acres. The Kansas Preservation Alliance gives the opening as June 1, 1879; Wikipedia says the hospital operated from 1872. Renamed Topeka State Hospital in 1901, it closed on May 17, 1997. The Topeka school district bought 152 acres and 30 buildings from the state in February 2009, and the five-storey Center Building, with its turrets and balconies, was pulled down in June 2010 with most of the rest.",
  "The Kirkbride building is gone and schools stand on much of the grounds. What is left to find is the hospital cemetery, 2.8 acres at the northeast corner of the old grounds about 100 yards west of the 100 block of NW MacVicar Avenue: of 1,157 graves only 16 have headstones. The legislature authorized a memorial with a plaque, a fence and the names of the dead in 1999-2000.",
  '','The pin is on the cemetery, which the Kansas Department of Administration took over when the hospital closed. Look from MacVicar Avenue; the rest of the grounds are school district land.',
  [W('Topeka State Hospital'),['Kansas Preservation Alliance: Topeka State Hospital','https://archives.kansaspreservationalliance.org/?p=83'],['Topeka & Shawnee County library: Topeka State Hospital','https://localhistory.tscpl.org/nodes/view/7120'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='John G. Haskell',archw='Wikipedia and the Kansas Preservation Alliance: "Architect John G. Haskell designed the buildings"'),
N('larned-state-hospital-ks','Larned State Hospital','Larned','ks','inst','standing',38.17373,-99.14823,'site','ask','drive','','1914–',
  "A state hospital opened on April 17, 1914 (the date is from Asylum Projects) on a 78-acre campus west of Larned, with its first patients sent from Topeka and Osawatomie. It was laid out on the cottage plan, grouping patients by condition, rather than as one great Kirkbride building, and the land round it was farmed. It is still a working state hospital, with the state's security hospital among its units.",
  "The Hospital building, one of the campus's early brick buildings, stands just north of the administration building across the street. Two rooms of its east wing and a corridor hold the hospital's museum of records, photographs and equipment, dedicated more than ten years after it was first proposed at the hospital's centennial in 2014, and named for a former superintendent.",
  '','A working state hospital, 1301 KS Highway 264. The museum is shown on guided tours on Fridays, arranged ahead with the superintendent\'s office, with no fee (Great Bend Tribune). Otherwise view from the road.',
  [['Great Bend Tribune: Larned State Hospital campus history','https://www.gbtribune.com/area-news/larned/campus-history/'],['Asylum Projects: Larned State Hospital (opening date)',AP+'Larned_State_Hospital']],
  brick='?'),
N('parsons-state-hospital-ks','Parsons State Hospital (State Hospital for Epileptics)','Parsons','ks','inst','standing',37.35138,-95.28095,'site','view','drive','','1903–',
  "Authorized in 1899 as a third Kansas asylum to relieve Topeka and Osawatomie; a lawsuit over the site let the first money lapse, and the hospital opened in October 1903 with more than 100 patients moved from those two hospitals. The legislature named it the State Hospital for Epileptics in 1905. Modelled on the epileptic colony at Sonyea, New York, it began with a dormitory and cottages and added an administration building, laundry, power plant and barn. Since 1957 it has served people with intellectual disabilities as Parsons State Hospital and Training Center.",
  "A working state hospital of 43 buildings on 163 acres. The sources read do not say which of the early buildings survive. Its cemetery holds about 700 to 800 former patients, most graves marked only with a small metal marker (Asylum Projects).",
  '','A working state hospital (Kansas Department for Aging and Disability Services) on North Boulevard. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Parsons State Hospital'),['Kansas: a Cyclopedia of State History (1912): Epileptic Hospital','https://ksgenweb-org.usgenwebsites.org/archives/1912/e/epileptic_hospital.html'],['Asylum Projects: Parsons State Hospital (cemetery)',AP+'Parsons_State_Hospital']],
  brick='?'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Nebraska
N('norfolk-state-hospital-ne','Norfolk State Hospital (Norfolk Regional Center)','Norfolk','ne','inst','gone',42.05076,-97.38437,'area','view','drive','','1886–',
  "Nebraska's second asylum, set up by the legislature in 1885 with $75,000 on 320 acres given by the town; the first building was finished in 1886 and the hospital opened in 1888 with 97 patients from Lincoln. A fire in 1901 destroyed all but one building, and it reopened in 1905 with cottages of brick and of stone and an administration building. Named the Norfolk State Hospital in 1920 and the Norfolk Regional Center in 1962, it held more than 1,300 patients at its peak.",
  "In 2006 the centre was turned to treating sex offenders, in a unit behind high-security fences. Wikipedia says that by 2010 many of the old buildings stood empty and some had been pulled down, and an editor's note on a travel article says the last of them came down in the summer of 2016; nothing was found to say any of the old hospital stands. Northeast Community College uses ground beside it. Asylum Projects says two hospital cemeteries are on the property.",
  '','The pin is on the regional centre, not on any one old building. An active secure state facility at 1700 North Victory Road: look from the public road only.',
  [W('Norfolk Regional Center'),['Asylum Projects: Norfolk State Hospital (buildings, cemeteries)',AP+'Norfolk_State_Hospital'],['Only In Your State: the asylum in Nebraska (note on the 2016 demolition)','https://www.onlyinyourstate.com/experiences/nebraska/norfolk-regional-center-ne']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('beatrice-feeble-minded-youth-farm-ne','Institution for Feeble Minded Youth Farm','Beatrice','ne','inst','standing',40.27468,-96.68917,'site','view','drive','','1935–',
  "The farm of the Nebraska Institution for Feeble Minded Youth, which the state created at Beatrice in 1885 and opened in 1887, now the Beatrice State Developmental Center a mile to the west. Built in 1935 with Public Works Administration money, it raised milk, meat and produce for the institution with the unpaid work of its residents, some of whom lived in a dormitory over the farm manager's quarters. The National Register form of 1997 calls it the only known surviving farm complex of a public institution, and the only one in Nebraska with a dormitory for residents doing farm work. It was used until the mid-1970s, sold in 1992 and listed in 1997.",
  "A two-storey brick and frame farmhouse and dormitory in the Dutch Colonial Revival style with a gambrel roof, a big gambrel-roofed dairy and hay barn with a clay tile roof and brick buttresses, a long frame chicken coop, and windbreaks and an allee of Austrian pine and cedar. The form says inside the house the original woodwork and the dormitory rooms of the upper floor survive. None of the institution's earlier barns stand.",
  '','A private home (the form of 1997 says it was a bed and breakfast in the 1990s, then the owners\' residence). Look from the country road only.',
  [['National Register record: Institution for Feeble Minded Youth Farm','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/97001521'],['National Register form (Park Service)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/7e149156-673a-4af8-8043-52d4ab08fb91'],['National Park Service: Institute for Feeble Minded Youth Farm','https://www.nps.gov/places/institute-for-feeble-minded-youth-farm.htm']],
  brick='?'),
]
